{
  "id": "bn-04",
  "kind": "lesson",
  "level": "A1",
  "order": 5,
  "title": "Nouns — Case and Animacy",
  "summary": "How Bengali nouns take genitive, objective and locative endings, and how animacy decides which endings are available; the definite articles টা/টি, গুলো and রা.",
  "rules": [
    {
      "id": "noun-animacy",
      "number": "28",
      "heading": "Three Classes of Noun",
      "statement": "For declension Bengali divides nouns by animacy: human beings, other living beings, and inanimate objects. The same case endings are available in principle, but not every ending is used with every class.",
      "examples": [
        {
          "target": "ছেলে",
          "roman": "chele",
          "gloss": "boy — human"
        },
        {
          "target": "কুকুর",
          "roman": "kukur",
          "gloss": "dog — other living"
        },
        {
          "target": "বই",
          "roman": "boi",
          "gloss": "book — inanimate"
        }
      ],
      "paradigms": [],
      "footnotes": [
        "Beames names the three classes vyakti-vācak, anyaprāṇ-vācak and aprāṇ-vācak."
      ],
      "seeAlso": [],
      "sources": [
        {
          "kind": "grammar",
          "citation": "Beames, John, *Grammar of the Bengali Language Literary and Colloquial*, Clarendon Press, Oxford, 1891.",
          "url": "https://archive.org/details/grammarofbengali00beamrich",
          "licence": "Public domain",
          "note": "Open scan on the Internet Archive."
        },
        {
          "kind": "grammar",
          "citation": "Wikipedia contributors, \"Bengali grammar\", *Wikipedia*, retrieved 13 August 2026.",
          "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bengali_grammar",
          "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
        }
      ],
      "core": true
    },
    {
      "id": "noun-genitive",
      "number": "29",
      "heading": "The Genitive",
      "statement": "The genitive adds **-ের** (-er) after a consonant or inherent অ, and **-র** (-r) after any other vowel. মাংস → মাংসের; ছেলে → ছেলের.",
      "examples": [
        {
          "target": "মাংসের",
          "roman": "maṅśer",
          "gloss": "of meat"
        },
        {
          "target": "ছেলের",
          "roman": "cheler",
          "gloss": "the boy's"
        },
        {
          "target": "নদীর",
          "roman": "nodir",
          "gloss": "of the river"
        }
      ],
      "paradigms": [],
      "footnotes": [],
      "seeAlso": [
        "noun-animacy"
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "kind": "grammar",
          "citation": "Wikipedia contributors, \"Bengali grammar\", *Wikipedia*, retrieved 13 August 2026.",
          "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bengali_grammar",
          "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
        },
        {
          "kind": "grammar",
          "citation": "Beames, John, *Grammar of the Bengali Language Literary and Colloquial*, Clarendon Press, Oxford, 1891.",
          "url": "https://archive.org/details/grammarofbengali00beamrich",
          "licence": "Public domain",
          "note": "Open scan on the Internet Archive."
        }
      ],
      "core": true
    },
    {
      "id": "noun-objective",
      "number": "30",
      "heading": "The Objective",
      "statement": "The objective (accusative/dative) ending **-কে** (-ke) is used for human beings. It is often omitted with other living beings and is not used with inanimates, which stay unmarked.",
      "examples": [
        {
          "target": "ছেলেকে",
          "roman": "cheleke",
          "gloss": "to/for the boy"
        },
        {
          "target": "বই",
          "roman": "boi",
          "gloss": "book — object, unmarked"
        }
      ],
      "paradigms": [],
      "footnotes": [
        "In Bangladesh **-রে** (-re) is also common for the same case."
      ],
      "seeAlso": [
        "noun-animacy"
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "kind": "grammar",
          "citation": "Wikipedia contributors, \"Bengali grammar\", *Wikipedia*, retrieved 13 August 2026.",
          "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bengali_grammar",
          "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
        },
        {
          "kind": "grammar",
          "citation": "Beames, John, *Grammar of the Bengali Language Literary and Colloquial*, Clarendon Press, Oxford, 1891.",
          "url": "https://archive.org/details/grammarofbengali00beamrich",
          "licence": "Public domain",
          "note": "Open scan on the Internet Archive."
        }
      ],
      "core": true
    },
    {
      "id": "noun-locative",
      "number": "31",
      "heading": "The Locative",
      "statement": "The locative marks place. After a consonant or inherent অ it is **-ে** (-e); after most vowels **-তে** (-te); after **-া** it is often **-য়** (-ẏ), as in কলকাতায়.",
      "examples": [
        {
          "target": "ঘরে",
          "roman": "ghore",
          "gloss": "in the room"
        },
        {
          "target": "নদীতে",
          "roman": "nodite",
          "gloss": "in/at the river"
        },
        {
          "target": "কলকাতায়",
          "roman": "kolkatay",
          "gloss": "in Kolkata"
        }
      ],
      "paradigms": [],
      "footnotes": [
        "Locative forms are not used with animate nouns in the ordinary way; place is inanimate."
      ],
      "seeAlso": [
        "noun-animacy",
        "noun-genitive"
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "kind": "grammar",
          "citation": "Wikipedia contributors, \"Bengali grammar\", *Wikipedia*, retrieved 13 August 2026.",
          "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bengali_grammar",
          "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
        }
      ],
      "core": true
    },
    {
      "id": "noun-definite",
      "number": "32",
      "heading": "Definite Articles",
      "statement": "Singular definite sense is marked by **-টা** (-ṭa) or formal **-টি** (-ṭi). Plural definite for inanimates uses **-গুলো** (-gulo); for humans **-রা** (-ra) is common.",
      "examples": [
        {
          "target": "বইটা",
          "roman": "boiṭa",
          "gloss": "the book"
        },
        {
          "target": "ছাত্রটি",
          "roman": "chatroṭi",
          "gloss": "the student (formal)"
        },
        {
          "target": "বইগুলো",
          "roman": "boigulo",
          "gloss": "the books"
        },
        {
          "target": "ছেলেরা",
          "roman": "chelera",
          "gloss": "the boys"
        }
      ],
      "paradigms": [],
      "footnotes": [],
      "seeAlso": [
        "noun-animacy"
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "kind": "grammar",
          "citation": "Wikipedia contributors, \"Bengali grammar\", *Wikipedia*, retrieved 13 August 2026.",
          "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bengali_grammar",
          "licence": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
        }
      ],
      "core": true
    },
    {
      "id": "noun-case-order",
      "number": "33",
      "heading": "Article Before Case",
      "statement": "When a definite article and a case ending both appear, the article comes first and the case ending follows it: ছাত্রটিকে, বইটার, জুতাগুলোতে.",
      "examples": [
        {
          "target": "ছাত্রটিকে",
          "roman": "chatroṭike",
          "gloss": "to the student"
        },
        {
          "target": "বইটার",
          "roman": "boiṭar",
          "gloss": "of the book"
        },
        {
          "target": "জুতাগুলোতে",
          "roman": "jutagulote",
          "gloss": "in/on the shoes"
        }
      ],
      "paradigms": [
